Read the diff, choose a commit or skip document, and submit it with the tool named below. Do not call bash or ask the user for confirmation, including “Would you like me to…”.

## WRITE THE MESSAGE FIRST

Write the message a senior maintainer would want in permanent history. Lead with why the change exists and its observable outcome. Explain the design decision when it is not obvious, call out breaking or operational consequences, and report only verification actually recorded here: {{ VERIFICATION_FACTS }}.

Detail target: **{{ MESSAGE_BUDGET }}**. Keep a narrow change to one short paragraph. Give a broad change a concise summary followed by a few cohesive paragraphs about behavior, design, migration risk, and verification. Never inventory files or directories, copy the diff, enumerate every edit, or pad the body with generated phrases such as “Changed X.”

## HOW TO WRITE THE SUBJECT

Summarize the change's primary intent, not its largest directory or the mechanics of the diff. Choose the conventional-commit type that best describes that intent: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `refactor`, `test`, `style`, `perf`, `build`, `ci`, or `chore`. Use a scope only when one stable product concept clearly owns the change; omit it for cross-cutting work. Mark a breaking change with `!`.

Write one concise imperative subject in the form `<kind>(<scope>)?!?: <description>`. Prefer language that completes “This commit will…”. Do not enumerate alternatives, end with punctuation, quote the frontmatter value, or use a ticket number as the description. The grammar requires lowercase type and scope, one space after the colon, and a description beginning with a lowercase letter or digit.

## HOW TO WRITE THE BODY

For any change beyond a one-line typo, formatting, or comment change, include one `## Body` item containing a self-contained commit body. Explain the problem or motivation first, then the behavioral or architectural outcome. Include migration risk or verification only when it helps a future maintainer understand or trust the change. Do not repeat the subject, narrate the diff, or inventory components. Use `## Body Summary`, `## Body Details`, and `## Body Footer` only when separation materially improves a complex migration, breaking change, or issue reference.

## Submit the result

The submission procedure below is the completion contract. For state changes, use {{ SUBMIT_MD_ARTIFACT_TOOL_INSTRUCTIONS }} and {{ DECLARE_COMPLETE_TOOL_REFERENCE }}.

## Document shape

Choose a commit document for pending work or a skip document when no commit is needed.

- Commit document: frontmatter `type: commit` and `subject:`. Include `## Body` (one concise self-contained item) or the detailed `## Body Summary` / `## Body Details` / `## Body Footer` set.

```markdown
---
type: commit
subject: fix(auth): prevent token expiry race
---

## Body Summary
- [BS-1] Prevents a race where refreshed tokens could expire mid-request.

## Body Details
- [BD-1] The expiry check now happens after refresh; covers the concurrent-refresh edge case.

```

- Skip document when no commit is needed:

```markdown
---
type: skip
reason: Only generated artifacts changed; nothing to commit.
---
```

